1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,175
July second, nineteen thirty seven.

2
00:00:02,175 --> 00:00:08,070
Aboard the Coast Guard cutter Itasca, anchored off Howland Island, in the middle of the Pacific.

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:11,017
Chief Radioman Leo Bellarts leans into his headset.

4
00:00:12,166 --> 00:00:15,019
He is listening for one specific aircraft.

5
00:00:15,019 --> 00:00:22,354
A silver, twin-engine Lockheed Electra, somewhere out over open water, flown by the most famous pilot in America.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,860
Her name is Amelia Earhart. Her navigator is Fred Noonan.

7
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:30,893
Together, they are trying to fly all the way around the world.

8
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:36,306
By this morning, they've already flown most of the way around the world.

9
00:00:36,306 --> 00:00:39,922
What's left is the single hardest leg of the entire trip.

10
00:00:41,066 --> 00:00:48,145
All morning, her voice has been coming through the radio, again and again, each transmission a little more urgent than the last.

11
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:53,652
We must be on you, but cannot see you. Gas is running low.

12
00:00:53,652 --> 00:00:58,713
We've been unable to reach you by radio. We are flying at a thousand feet.

13
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:05,162
At oh eight forty three, the final transmission anyone would ever receive from that aircraft comes through the speaker.

14
00:01:06,299 --> 00:01:10,237
We are on the line one five seven, three three seven.

15
00:01:10,237 --> 00:01:16,323
We will repeat this message on six two one oh kilocycles. We are running north and south.

16
00:01:17,466 --> 00:01:22,294
Here is the detail almost nobody gets right about this moment. That signal was not fading.

17
00:01:22,294 --> 00:01:26,217
It was exactly as loud and clear as it had been all morning.

18
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:29,579
Bellarts himself put it plainly, decades later.

19
00:01:29,579 --> 00:01:36,848
She was so loud, he said, that he ran up to the bridge, expecting to see her plane coming in for a landing.

20
00:01:37,966 --> 00:01:41,336
Nobody ever did see it. After that transmission: silence.

21
00:01:41,336 --> 00:01:46,952
To this day, nobody knows exactly what happened to Amelia Earhart, or to Fred Noonan.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,216
You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

23
00:01:51,333 --> 00:01:52,639
Amelia Earhart.

24
00:01:53,766 --> 00:01:55,787
One honest rule before we start.

25
00:01:55,787 --> 00:02:02,858
Every timestamp and quote in this episode comes from the National Archives' own radio log, or from a named, attributed source.

26
00:02:02,858 --> 00:02:12,287
Where something is a theory and not a proven fact — and in this case, there are several — we will say so, on screen, every single time.

27
00:02:13,433 --> 00:02:19,232
To understand why an entire ocean got searched for this one woman, you have to understand who she already was.

28
00:02:20,366 --> 00:02:32,443
In June of nineteen twenty eight, Amelia Earhart became the first woman to cross the Atlantic by air — as a passenger, aboard a Fokker trimotor named the Friendship, flown by pilot Wilmer Stultz and mechanic Louis Gordon.

29
00:02:32,443 --> 00:02:37,528
She didn't fly it herself. The fame landed on her anyway, and it never really left.

30
00:02:38,666 --> 00:02:40,960
Four years later, she fixed that herself.

31
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:50,134
In May of nineteen thirty two, she flew a red Lockheed Vega solo across the Atlantic — only the second person ever to do it, after Charles Lindbergh.

32
00:02:51,266 --> 00:03:04,745
Three years after that, in January of nineteen thirty five, she became the first person ever to fly solo across the Pacific — Honolulu to Oakland, twenty four hundred miles, a route that had already killed ten aviators who'd tried it before her.

33
00:03:05,866 --> 00:03:14,539
That same year, Purdue University's president recruited her as a visiting faculty member, with a documented official title: Counselor on Careers for Women.

34
00:03:15,666 --> 00:03:22,031
A Purdue trustee named David Ross helped raise money for something called the Amelia Earhart Fund for Aeronautical Research.

35
00:03:22,031 --> 00:03:27,055
Between his gift and a handful of corporate sponsors, it totaled around eighty thousand dollars.

36
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:36,641
The plane that money helped buy was publicly billed as a flying laboratory — new radio gear, new navigation instruments, wing de-icing equipment.

37
00:03:36,641 --> 00:03:42,881
How much of that was a real research program, and how much was publicity, is genuinely disputed.

38
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:57,061
She took delivery of it from Lockheed's Burbank plant in July of nineteen thirty six — a Lockheed Electra ten E, twin-engine, all metal, fifty five feet across the wings, with ten fuel tanks holding over eleven hundred gallons combined.

39
00:03:57,061 --> 00:04:01,633
Far more range than a stock Electra that size was ever built to carry.

40
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:07,841
For a flight with no landmarks over open ocean, she needed a navigator who could handle it.

41
00:04:07,841 --> 00:04:09,035
She chose Fred Noonan.

42
00:04:10,166 --> 00:04:14,250
Noonan had spent years as a merchant seaman before he ever flew.

43
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:23,097
By the early nineteen thirties, he was one of Pan American Airways' leading oceanic navigators — the man who helped chart their first trans-Pacific Clipper routes.

44
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:27,352
Why Noonan actually left Pan Am is genuinely disputed.

45
00:04:27,352 --> 00:04:31,165
One account says he resigned to start his own navigation school.

46
00:04:31,165 --> 00:04:38,444
A separate, later account alleges he was let go over drinking — a claim that didn't surface publicly until decades afterward.

47
00:04:39,566 --> 00:04:45,597
On March twenty seventh, nineteen thirty seven, Noonan married Mary Martinelli, a hairdresser from Oakland.

48
00:04:45,597 --> 00:04:48,813
It was roughly three months before he disappeared.

49
00:04:49,933 --> 00:04:54,609
Getting into the air, even once, almost ended this flight before it began.

50
00:04:55,733 --> 00:05:00,178
The first attempt left Oakland on March seventeenth, nineteen thirty seven.

51
00:05:00,178 --> 00:05:08,664
Earhart, Noonan, a second navigator named Harry Manning, and a technical advisor named Paul Mantz — flying west, Oakland toward Honolulu.

52
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:21,166
Three days later, taking off again from Luke Field on Ford Island, the heavily loaded Electra ground-looped on the runway — a blown tire, uneven engine power, the exact cause disputed even then.

53
00:05:21,166 --> 00:05:22,199
Nobody was hurt.

54
00:05:22,199 --> 00:05:28,399
The airplane went back to Lockheed's Burbank plant for repair, and the whole plan was rebuilt from scratch.

55
00:05:29,533 --> 00:05:34,627
When it flew again, the route ran the opposite direction, partly for the season's winds.

56
00:05:34,627 --> 00:05:39,721
Manning and Mantz didn't continue. From here on, Noonan was the only navigator on board.

57
00:05:40,866 --> 00:05:49,617
The Electra left Oakland again on May twenty first, nineteen thirty seven, working its way toward Miami for one final overhaul before the real flight began.

58
00:05:50,766 --> 00:05:55,556
On June first, nineteen thirty seven, the official world flight departure leaves Miami.

59
00:05:55,556 --> 00:05:57,767
Crowds gather to watch it go.

60
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:15,253
From Miami, the route runs down through the Caribbean and South America — San Juan, Caripito in Venezuela, Paramaribo in Suriname, Brazil — then, from Natal, the jump across open ocean: the South Atlantic crossing, landing next in Saint-Louis, Senegal.

61
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:26,588
Then west to east across the width of Africa — Dakar, Gao in Mali, Fort-Lamy in Chad, El Fasher and Khartoum in Sudan, Massawa and Assab on the Red Sea.

62
00:06:27,733 --> 00:06:41,369
Then across Asia — Karachi, Calcutta, Rangoon, Bangkok, Singapore — and on through Java, Timor, and Darwin, Australia, until, on June twenty ninth, they touch down at Lae, in New Guinea.

63
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:47,314
By Lae, they have flown twenty two thousand of a planned twenty nine thousand miles.

64
00:06:47,314 --> 00:06:58,225
What's left is the leg nobody else on this trip has come close to matching: two thousand, five hundred and fifty six miles of open Pacific, to a speck of an island called Howland.

65
00:06:59,366 --> 00:07:02,083
July first was supposed to be the day.

66
00:07:02,083 --> 00:07:04,800
Wrong-direction winds and threatening cloud scrubbed the takeoff.

67
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:06,837
They tried again the next morning.

68
00:07:07,966 --> 00:07:19,695
At around ten A M local time on July second, the heavily loaded Electra rolled down a grass airstrip barely three thousand feet long — one that ended at a bluff, dropping into a shark-populated gulf.

69
00:07:20,833 --> 00:07:28,539
Ground crew who watched it happen remembered the plane could barely lift off — using, in their own words, every inch of runway and then some.

70
00:07:29,666 --> 00:07:33,939
A Guinea Airways employee named Alan Board took a photograph of that takeoff.

71
00:07:33,939 --> 00:07:37,555
It remains the last confirmed image ever taken of that aircraft.

72
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:40,452
One detail is easy to miss.

73
00:07:40,452 --> 00:07:55,053
The day before, Noonan hadn't been able to set his marine chronometers against a radio time signal, because of a radio problem at Lae — which meant the entire twenty five hundred mile crossing began with a small timing error already built in, before the wheels ever left the ground.

74
00:07:56,200 --> 00:08:01,111
This brings us back to where this episode started — the radio shack aboard the Itasca.

75
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:10,514
The Coast Guard cutter Itasca was stationed off Howland Island specifically to guide the Electra in by radio, for the final approach of the entire round-the-world flight.

76
00:08:11,633 --> 00:08:15,329
About two hundred miles out. Please take a bearing on us.

77
00:08:16,466 --> 00:08:18,499
About one hundred miles out now.

78
00:08:19,633 --> 00:08:29,194
At oh seven forty two, the transmission you heard at the start of this episode comes through — gas running low, flying at a thousand feet, unable to reach Itasca by radio.

79
00:08:30,333 --> 00:08:33,019
We are circling, but cannot hear you.

80
00:08:34,166 --> 00:08:37,660
We received your signals, but unable to get a minimum.

81
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:43,250
Please take a bearing on us, and answer on three thousand one hundred five, with voice.

82
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:51,166
Forty minutes later, at oh eight forty three, comes the transmission that would end up being the last one anyone ever received.

83
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:54,049
This is the actual document.

84
00:08:54,049 --> 00:09:02,096
The Itasca's own radio log, written down in real time, on July second, nineteen thirty seven — held today at the National Archives.

85
00:09:03,233 --> 00:09:10,363
The now-familiar wording of that final message — the version usually quoted in full — is a cleaned-up, typed reconstruction.

86
00:09:10,363 --> 00:09:15,354
The raw handwritten log underneath it is terser, and in places, harder to read.

87
00:09:16,500 --> 00:09:20,285
We are on the line one five seven, three three seven.

88
00:09:20,285 --> 00:09:26,135
We will repeat this message on six two one oh kilocycles. We are running north and south.

89
00:09:27,266 --> 00:09:32,673
After that — nothing. No further transmission, ever again, from that aircraft.

90
00:09:33,799 --> 00:09:39,833
Somewhere over open water, past the point anyone could still hear her, the Electra's engines were running on fumes.

91
00:09:40,966 --> 00:09:43,442
Even the official record isn't perfectly clean.

92
00:09:43,442 --> 00:09:54,053
Itasca's own commanding officer, Commander Warner Thompson, later added details to his written report — like the weather being cloudy and overcast — that simply aren't in the original log.

93
00:09:55,199 --> 00:10:02,396
That one five seven, three three seven line is a compass heading, from a sun-line navigation technique Noonan used.

94
00:10:02,396 --> 00:10:07,320
Mathematically, it describes a line running northwest to southeast, passing near Howland Island.

95
00:10:08,466 --> 00:10:21,057
According to later celestial-navigation analysis, that same line also happens to pass near a second island, roughly three hundred fifty nautical miles southeast of Howland — a speck of coral called Gardner Island.

96
00:10:22,199 --> 00:10:25,461
That the line intersects Gardner Island is simple math.

97
00:10:25,461 --> 00:10:30,896
That Earhart and Noonan actually flew it there, and landed, is not a confirmed fact.

98
00:10:30,896 --> 00:10:33,432
It's a theory. We'll get to it.

99
00:10:34,566 --> 00:10:40,679
When Itasca got no further contact, the United States launched one of the largest search operations in its history.

100
00:10:41,799 --> 00:10:50,080
The battleship USS Colorado, on a training cruise near Hawaii, was diverted, and launched three scout floatplanes to search.

101
00:10:51,199 --> 00:11:02,510
Colorado's planes searched Winslow Reef, found nothing there at all, then flew over McKean Island — uninhabited, in the pilots' own words, except for, quote, myriads of birds.

102
00:11:03,633 --> 00:11:09,779
Then they flew over Gardner Island — the same island that line of position happens to pass through.

103
00:11:09,779 --> 00:11:13,194
The lead pilot, Lieutenant John Lambrecht, filed an official report.

104
00:11:14,333 --> 00:11:22,216
His report, on the record: signs of recent habitation were clearly visible, but repeated circling and zooming failed to elicit any answering wave.

105
00:11:22,216 --> 00:11:25,644
It was finally taken for granted that none was there.

106
00:11:26,766 --> 00:11:30,702
What that quote actually means for Earhart's fate is not something Lambrecht's report claims.

107
00:11:30,702 --> 00:11:34,639
That reading of it belongs to a theory we'll cover later in this episode.

108
00:11:34,639 --> 00:11:37,450
The quote itself is real. The meaning is not settled.

109
00:11:38,599 --> 00:11:44,887
Colorado's planes also swept Hull, Sydney, Phoenix, Enderbury, Birnie and Canton Islands.

110
00:11:44,887 --> 00:11:47,507
Every one of them, negative.

111
00:11:48,633 --> 00:11:59,214
The aircraft carrier USS Lexington joined next, with sixty two planes aboard, sweeping over one hundred thousand square miles northwest of Howland in grid patterns two miles apart.

112
00:11:59,214 --> 00:12:05,639
Every single mission log from that search records some version of the same two words: nothing sighted.

113
00:12:06,766 --> 00:12:11,980
The search was called off on July nineteenth, nineteen thirty seven. Sixteen days.

114
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:15,991
About nine ships. Sixty six aircraft. Roughly four thousand people.

115
00:12:15,991 --> 00:12:20,402
Over four million dollars — close to eighty eight million today.

116
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:26,717
No wreckage. No oil slick. No debris of any kind, ever recovered.

117
00:12:26,717 --> 00:12:32,766
The Navy's official, contemporaneous conclusion: the Electra ran out of fuel, crashed, and sank.

118
00:12:33,899 --> 00:12:40,952
That finding — lost at sea, no wreckage ever found — remains the United States government's official position to this day.

119
00:12:40,952 --> 00:12:43,303
No US agency has ever revised it.

120
00:12:44,433 --> 00:12:48,194
One more official act followed, separately, eighteen months later.

121
00:12:48,194 --> 00:13:01,987
On January fifth, nineteen thirty nine, a Los Angeles County court granted Amelia Earhart's legal death declaration, at her husband George Putnam's request — granted unusually fast, which the judge himself called extraordinary.

122
00:13:03,133 --> 00:13:08,932
For nearly ninety years, one theory has outlasted every other explanation for what happened next.

123
00:13:10,066 --> 00:13:20,097
It belongs to a research group called Tighar — the International Group for Historic Aircraft Recovery — and specifically to its founder and executive director, Ric Gillespie.

124
00:13:21,233 --> 00:13:25,052
Gillespie's theory: Earhart and Noonan didn't crash at sea at all.

125
00:13:25,052 --> 00:13:31,995
They flew that one five seven, three three seven line southeast, and landed on Gardner Island — today called Nikumaroro.

126
00:13:33,133 --> 00:13:39,011
Since nineteen eighty eight, Tighar has run around a dozen expeditions to that island, looking for proof.

127
00:13:40,133 --> 00:13:43,232
What they've recovered: a fragment of aircraft-grade aluminum.

128
00:13:43,232 --> 00:13:45,944
Part of a nineteen thirties woman's shoe.

129
00:13:45,944 --> 00:13:50,980
A broken cosmetic jar, consistent with a popular freckle ointment of the era.

130
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:54,466
A mirror compact. A pocketknife. An empty liquor bottle.

131
00:13:54,466 --> 00:13:58,340
A sextant box, since lost, with numbers stamped on it.

132
00:13:59,466 --> 00:14:06,953
Every single one of those items is described, carefully, as consistent with the era, or consistent with what Earhart or Noonan might have carried.

133
00:14:06,953 --> 00:14:10,385
Not one of them has ever been proven to be theirs.

134
00:14:11,533 --> 00:14:19,518
Remember Lieutenant Lambrecht's report, from that same island, in nineteen thirty seven — signs of recent habitation, but no one answering their wave?

135
00:14:19,518 --> 00:14:22,295
This is exactly the line Tighar points to.

136
00:14:23,433 --> 00:14:26,994
It's a real quote, from a real official report.

137
00:14:26,994 --> 00:14:34,117
Whether it actually describes castaways, rather than something else entirely, is Gillespie's interpretation — not a Navy finding.

138
00:14:35,266 --> 00:14:40,830
In nineteen forty, three years after the disappearance, something was actually found on that island.

139
00:14:41,966 --> 00:14:48,888
A British colonial officer named Gerald Gallagher discovered thirteen human bones near an old campfire site on Nikumaroro.

140
00:14:50,033 --> 00:14:55,102
The bones were sent to Fiji for examination. A doctor named D. W.

141
00:14:55,102 --> 00:14:59,782
Hoodless studied them, and concluded they belonged to a stocky, middle-aged man.

142
00:14:59,782 --> 00:15:00,952
Not Amelia Earhart.

143
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:07,334
Tighar rediscovered Hoodless's original report in the late nineteen nineties, and challenged his methods.

144
00:15:07,334 --> 00:15:14,063
In two thousand eighteen, a forensic anthropologist named Richard Jantz ran the same original measurements through modern software.

145
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:23,279
His statistical conclusion: those measurements were more consistent with Earhart than with ninety nine percent of a large reference population.

146
00:15:23,279 --> 00:15:26,510
Peer-reviewed — but a probability, not an identification.

147
00:15:27,633 --> 00:15:36,642
A separate two thousand fifteen paper, by researchers named Cross and Wright, defended Hoodless's original nineteen forty finding as methodologically sound.

148
00:15:36,642 --> 00:15:38,787
The two studies genuinely disagree.

149
00:15:39,933 --> 00:15:42,706
Here's the complication that will never go away.

150
00:15:42,706 --> 00:15:46,173
The bones themselves have been lost since the nineteen forties.

151
00:15:46,173 --> 00:15:50,332
No new physical test, no DNA — nothing — is possible anymore.

152
00:15:50,332 --> 00:15:52,759
It's a permanent argument over eighty-year-old measurements.

153
00:15:53,899 --> 00:16:04,818
In two thousand nineteen, National Geographic sent forensic anthropologist Erin Kimmerle to examine those same measurements again, and to attempt DNA testing against Earhart's own relatives.

154
00:16:05,966 --> 00:16:14,185
Her reconstructed skull was consistent with an adult female — matching, among other things, one of Earhart's known sinus features.

155
00:16:14,185 --> 00:16:19,939
But the DNA testing itself came back inconclusive. The samples were simply too degraded.

156
00:16:19,939 --> 00:16:22,815
Not a match. Not a non-match. Inconclusive.

157
00:16:23,933 --> 00:16:29,497
Not every theory about this case has survived scrutiny. Two of them collapsed completely.

158
00:16:30,633 --> 00:16:35,821
The first: that Earhart and Noonan were captured by Japan, and held somewhere in the Pacific.

159
00:16:35,821 --> 00:16:40,037
It's circulated since the nineteen sixties, with no solid documentary evidence behind it.

160
00:16:41,166 --> 00:16:49,290
In July of twenty seventeen, the History Channel aired a photograph it said showed Earhart and Noonan in Japanese custody, on an atoll called Jaluit.

161
00:16:50,433 --> 00:16:59,428
Within about a week, a Japanese blogger named Kota Yamano found the identical photograph — published in a Japanese travel book from nineteen thirty five.

162
00:16:59,428 --> 00:17:01,587
Two years before Earhart ever disappeared.

163
00:17:02,733 --> 00:17:05,076
That specific photo is conclusively false.

164
00:17:05,076 --> 00:17:10,935
And no record since has ever produced credible evidence for the wider Japanese-capture theory either.

165
00:17:12,066 --> 00:17:14,732
The second collapsed theory is much more recent.

166
00:17:14,732 --> 00:17:23,064
In January of twenty twenty four, a company called Deep Sea Vision announced sonar imagery that looked like a twin-engine aircraft, on the Pacific floor.

167
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:25,796
It made headlines everywhere.

168
00:17:25,796 --> 00:17:34,577
Ten months later, the same team revisited the site, and identified the shape as a natural rock formation — not the Electra.

169
00:17:34,577 --> 00:17:41,361
The company's own CEO, Tony Romeo, later admitted, self-deprecatingly, that it probably wasn't the plane after all.

170
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:47,019
As recently as this decade, this case has kept generating fresh headlines.

171
00:17:48,166 --> 00:17:56,055
In September of twenty twenty five, the US government ordered the declassification of every Earhart-related government record still held back.

172
00:17:57,200 --> 00:18:07,388
The National Archives began releasing pages in waves — four thousand six hundred and twenty four pages that September, then thousands more across November, December, and January.

173
00:18:08,533 --> 00:18:24,259
What's actually in them: final radio transmissions, weather data, search records, internal Navy investigations — and some genuine period ephemera, like a self-described nineteen thirty seven, quote, telepath, claiming psychic knowledge of the crash.

174
00:18:25,399 --> 00:18:33,004
A television host named Ross Coulthart covered the release, framing the documents as challenging the official search's, quote, tunnel vision.

175
00:18:33,004 --> 00:18:35,665
That's real journalism, on a real release.

176
00:18:36,799 --> 00:18:41,877
But no outlet, anywhere, has reported an actual smoking-gun finding inside those documents.

177
00:18:41,877 --> 00:18:44,611
As of this recording, there isn't one.

178
00:18:45,733 --> 00:18:56,104
Then there's the Taraia Object — a shape on the floor of Nikumaroro's own lagoon, first spotted on satellite imagery back in twenty twenty, by a retired Navy veteran named Mike Ashmore.

179
00:18:57,233 --> 00:19:01,505
CNN gave it fresh attention in October of twenty twenty five.

180
00:19:01,505 --> 00:19:08,884
An organization called the Archaeological Legacy Institute, led by Richard Pettigrew, partnered with Purdue University to plan an expedition.

181
00:19:10,033 --> 00:19:12,281
That expedition has already been postponed twice.

182
00:19:12,281 --> 00:19:18,706
As of this recording, it has not left port, and it has found nothing — because it hasn't gone yet.

183
00:19:19,833 --> 00:19:28,529
Even Ric Gillespie — the man who's spent decades chasing the Nikumaroro theory — has publicly said he sees nothing there consistent with wreckage.

184
00:19:28,529 --> 00:19:31,066
His own guess: a downed coconut tree.

185
00:19:32,199 --> 00:19:34,249
One more thing worth saying plainly.

186
00:19:34,249 --> 00:19:39,714
A video went viral in January of twenty twenty six, claiming the mystery was finally solved.

187
00:19:39,714 --> 00:19:42,447
No primary source anywhere backs that claim up.

188
00:19:42,447 --> 00:19:45,521
As of this recording, the case remains officially unresolved.

189
00:19:46,666 --> 00:19:49,500
Take stock of everything this case actually has.

190
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:52,688
A radio log with one loud, urgent, final transmission.

191
00:19:52,688 --> 00:19:56,584
A search the size of Texas that found nothing at all.

192
00:19:56,584 --> 00:20:01,189
A navigational line that happens to touch an island. Bones nobody can retest.

193
00:20:01,189 --> 00:20:04,377
And ninety years of theories, none of them proven.

194
00:20:05,499 --> 00:20:17,463
That combination — real evidence, genuinely inconclusive, sitting right next to a story people desperately want an ending for — is exactly why this case has never stopped generating headlines.

195
00:20:18,599 --> 00:20:23,588
In October of twenty twenty six, another expedition returns to that same lagoon.

196
00:20:24,733 --> 00:20:38,876
If a story that ends in total silence unsettled you, wait until you see what really happened to Malaysia Airlines flight three seventy — a Boeing that vanished the exact same way, seventy seven years later, with radar data this time, and still no wreckage.

197
00:20:38,876 --> 00:20:44,534
That's our episode, M H three seventy, The Plane That Vanished From Earth, right here on this channel.

198
00:20:45,666 --> 00:20:50,107
Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, for the investigations everyone else got wrong.

